\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

All real-model numbers are for the pool of Section~\ref{sec:method}: 1500 MMLU-Pro items, 128 configurations of Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, mean configuration accuracy $0.274$ (range $0.205$--$0.303$), true spread $\sigmatheta=0.014$ (between-pool interval $0.012$--$0.019$), mean pairwise item-level correlation of configurations $0.66$.
Intervals are between-pool intervals unless stated; a standard error of the fresh-item accuracy is $0.012$--$0.014$.
The synthetic arm has exact truth and is used where the real matrix cannot vary the factor.

\subsection{How large is the gap?}
\label{sec:size}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../figures/fig1.pdf}
\caption{Size of the gap on the real pool (1500 MMLU-Pro items, 128 configurations of Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct). Left: search, report, verify. Right: random search over $B$ configurations on $n$ items, truth the accuracy on the pool items outside $S$; $2000$ draws of $S$ per cell. Top: accuracy reported on $S$ and fresh accuracy of the selected configuration ($B=100$), with the fresh accuracy of the default, of the mean and of the best configuration of the pool. Bottom: gap for $B=5,20,100$; bands are $95\%$ between-pool intervals, the grey band $\pm1.96$ standard errors of one fresh-item accuracy. Open markers: $n=400$, where $S$ is $27\%$ of the pool (whole-pool reading of the gap $0.018$ against $0.025$ for $B=100$).}
\label{fig:size}
\end{figure}

Searching $B=100$ of the 128 configurations on $n=30$ items reports $0.390$ for a configuration whose accuracy on fresh items is $0.277$ (gap $0.113$); the gap falls to $0.077$ at $n=60$, $0.059$ at $100$, $0.038$ at $200$ and $0.025$ at $400$ (Figure~\ref{fig:size}).
It grows with the number of configurations evaluated: at $n=60$ it is $0.036$, $0.058$, $0.077$ and $0.079$ for $5$, $20$, $100$ and all $128$.
All 35 cells (seven searchers, five sizes) have a between-pool interval that excludes zero.
At $n=400$ the gap against the items outside $S$ exceeds the whole-pool reading by $N/(N-n)$, and the point is flagged.

The size has to be read against what tuning delivers.
The default configuration scores $0.262$ on fresh items, the selected one $0.277$ at $n=30$ and $0.287$ at $n=400$, the average configuration $0.274$ and the best configuration of the pool $0.303$.
A reader of the reported scores sees an improvement over the default of $0.125$ at $n=30$, $0.095$ at $60$ and $0.049$ at $400$, against fresh improvements of $0.015$, $0.017$ and $0.025$: for $n\le100$ the reported improvement is at least four times the real one.

\subsection{What drives the gap?}
\label{sec:drivers}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../figures/fig2.pdf}
\caption{Drivers of the gap. (a) Real pool: measured gap against the search-log prediction $\kappa\bar\sigma/\sqrt n$ for exhaustive search over $8$, $32$ or $128$ configurations drawn from the whole pool, its central half or its densest window, $n\in\{60,120,240\}$ (shade: true spread over the standard error of one configuration), and for the cells of Figure~\ref{fig:size} (open); $95\%$ between-pool intervals. (b) Synthetic worlds with exact truth ($504$ cells, $4$ worlds $\times150$ draws; intervals over worlds in the source file). (c) Real pool, $n=60$: gap against configurations evaluated by each searcher.}
\label{fig:drivers}
\end{figure}

The prediction $\kappa\bar\sigma/\sqrt n$ from the search log alone is $0.95$, $1.02$, $1.06$, $1.16$ and $1.26$ times the measured gap of exhaustive search at $n=30,\dots,400$: it matches within $6\%$ for $n\le100$ and overshoots by $16\%$ at $n=200$ and $26\%$ at $n=400$, where $S$ is $27\%$ of the pool (Figure~\ref{fig:drivers}a). Across the $15$ cells of exhaustive and random ($B=20,100$) search the slope of the measured gap on the prediction is $1.15$ ($1.09$--$1.19$), so by the rule fixed in advance (slope interval containing $1$) it does not track the gap, though its mean error ($0.005$) is inside the tolerance ($0.015$) and the prediction is an upper end of the gap in $80\%$ of the cells.
Over subsets of the space and $n\in\{60,120,240\}$ (21 cells) the slope of the gap on the prediction is $1.01$ ($0.92$--$1.08$) with mean error $0.0025$ against a tolerance of $0.011$, so it tracks; the ratio is $0.93$--$1.01$ in every cell where $\sigmatheta$ is below $3.2$ standard errors of one configuration and $1.07$--$1.32$ where it is above $3.5$ (correlation over 21 cells $0.85$).
$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ is $7.9$--$8.5$ for $K=128$, $4.0$--$4.2$ for $B=20$ and $7.2$--$7.8$ for $B=100$: the configurations are correlated and compete like about eight independent ones.
In the synthetic arm, where $n$, $K$ and $\sigmatheta$ are set, the gap rises with $K$, falls with $n$ and, at large $n$, with $\sigmatheta$ (at $K=128$, $n=800$: $0.047$ at $\sigmatheta=0$, $0.022$ at $0.04$); the prediction is within $7\%$ at $\sigmatheta=0$ and overshoots by $20$--$30\%$ at $n=400$ and $60$--$75\%$ at $n=800$ when $\sigmatheta>0$ (Figure~\ref{fig:drivers}b).

At equal evaluation budget exhaustive search is not distinguishable from random search over $100$ configurations (paired difference $+0.002$, interval $-0.0004$ to $0.0046$ at $n=60$), hill climbing, which visits $18$ configurations on average, matches random search over $20$, and successive halving reports less optimism ($0.062$ against $0.077$ at $100$ evaluations; paired difference $-0.016$, interval $-0.022$ to $-0.010$); its final choice is between two survivors (Figure~\ref{fig:drivers}c).
Putting a search item among the demonstrations raises the probability of answering it correctly by $\Delta_{\mathrm{leak}}=0.42$ (interval $0.22$--$0.62$ over nine probe items), so a search that places $k$ of its $n$ items in the prompt overstates by about $(k/n)\Delta_{\mathrm{leak}}$: $0.042$ for three demonstrations at $n=30$.

\subsection{Do reported scores mislead decisions?}
\label{sec:decisions}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../figures/fig3.pdf}
\caption{Decisions from reported scores. (a) Synthetic worlds in which all $128$ configurations are equally accurate: rate of declaring that tuning helped over the default configuration (one-sided $5\%$ rules; $B$ configurations evaluated; intervals over $6$ worlds $\times500$ draws). (b) Real pool: the rate (over all draws) of declaring a help although the tuned configuration is not better than the default on the items outside $S$, for all $128$ configurations and for the $74$ configurations within $0.02$ of each other in accuracy (a near-null, not an exact null); $B=100$, intervals from $40$ resampled pools and therefore coarse. (c) Two independent searches, $A$ with $100$ and $B$ with $20$ (5:1) or both $20$ (1:1) evaluations: fraction of draws in which the reported score favours $A$.}
\label{fig:decisions}
\end{figure}

\emph{Tuned versus untuned.}
On synthetic worlds in which all configurations are exactly equally accurate and $n=120$, the naive test declares that tuning helped in $32\%$ of draws when $20$ configurations are evaluated and $54\%$ when $100$ are, against a nominal $5\%$; the max-t test declares in $1.6\%$ and $4.5\%$ and the held-out test in $6\%$ (Figure~\ref{fig:decisions}).
On the real pool tuning does help on average (mean fresh gain $+0.019$), so the naive test declares a help in $68\%$ of draws at $n=120$ with $100$ evaluations; what it overstates is the size.
In $4.5\%$ of all draws (interval $0$--$0.22$) the naive test declares a help although the selected configuration is not better than the default on fresh items; max-t does so in $1.1\%$ and the held-out test in $0.8\%$ (such draws are $6.2\%$ of the total, so these are joint rates, not sizes).
In the densest window of $74$ configurations within $0.02$ in accuracy, a near-null and not an exact null, the rates are $9.7\%$ (naive), $1.4\%$ (max-t) and $1.5\%$ (held-out) of all draws, $14.9\%$ of which have a non-positive gain; no excess over nominal is claimed.

\emph{Tuned versus tuned.}
At $n=120$, when system~A searches $100$ configurations and system~B $20$, the larger search has the higher reported score in $74\%$ of draws on the synthetic exact null and in $57\%$ (own search sets) to $71\%$ (shared set) on the real pool, although the true difference of the deployed configurations is zero ($0.0005$ on the real pool); at equal budgets ($20$ against $20$, own search sets) it is $45\%$ and $47\%$, and on a shared set $40\%$ and $37\%$, because equal reported scores do not count.
The significance test rarely fires ($2\%$ synthetic, $2$--$5\%$ real), so the distortion is in the direction of the reported comparison, not in the size of the test.

