At $N_{\mathrm{tot}}=120$ and random search over $100$ configurations the naive report exceeds the fresh accuracy of the deployed configuration in $91\%$ of draws (bias $+0.052$); the optimism bootstrap does in $62\%$ ($+0.014$) and the .632 variant in $66\%$ ($+0.017$); cross-fit and the held-out splits are near $50\%$ (cross-fit estimates the mean accuracy of its fold-trained configurations, not the deployed one) (Figure~\ref{fig:protocols}, Table~\ref{tab:protocols}).
Holding out items costs little here: the deployed configuration of a 20\% or 50\% split is $0.0007$ or $0.0025$ less accurate than the one the naive protocol deploys, inside the noise.

Among lower bounds over the declared space, the max-t bound covers $97.0\%$ (interval $93.3$--$99.4$) with mean width $0.069$ at $N_{\mathrm{tot}}=120$ and $95.7\%$ ($92.5$--$98.1$), width $0.050$, at $240$; Bonferroni and \v{S}id\'ak cover $99.5\%$ at $120$ and $98.9\%$ at $240$ with widths $0.092$ and $0.066$; the held-out bounds cover $96.0$--$96.7\%$ at $120$ with widths $0.092$ (50\%) and $0.140$ (20\%), and the unadjusted bound on the selected configuration covers $69\%$.
On the real pool the max-t bound is therefore valid in every primary random-search cell and the narrowest valid bound, and, unlike the held-out bounds, it spends no items on reporting.

\emph{Where the cheap bound fails.}
The bound is valid for a fixed candidate set, but under adaptive search the choice of that set matters (Figure~\ref{fig:coverage}).
On the real pool, over the declared space, it covers $96$--$99\%$ for random search, hill climbing and successive halving at $N_{\mathrm{tot}}=120$ and $240$; in the synthetic worlds with no spread in true accuracy ($\sigma_\theta=0$) it holds at $N_{\mathrm{tot}}=120$ ($95.6\%$ for random search over $100$) but falls to $92.4\%$ ($91.3$--$93.6$) at $240$, and with spread ($\sigma_\theta=0.01$, $0.03$) it is at least $96\%$. Where it undercovers it is also narrow.
Over the configurations the search evaluated it covers $95$--$97\%$ for random search and $94\%$ ($88$--$97$) and $92\%$ ($88$--$96$) for hill climbing at $120$ and $240$ (inconclusive: the conservative scoring gives $0.98$--$0.99$), but only $81$--$86\%$ for successive halving, whose evaluated set is the two configurations that reach the last round; the conservative scoring gives $0.93$--$0.96$, so the bound is invalid there.
The synthetic arm shows the same pattern at $N_{\mathrm{tot}}=120$ ($95\%$, $95\%$ and $48$--$65\%$) and a weaker one at $240$ ($91\%$, $93\%$ and $40$--$58\%$).
A bound that is simultaneous over what was evaluated is valid only if that set was fixed before the data were seen; after adaptive elimination only the declared space is.

